proof.text:
For a polynomial monomial $a\prod_i x_i^{
u_i}$, expand $a\prod_i(y_i+z_i)^{
u_i}$ by multiplying the finite collection of factors. The term choosing no $z$ is $a\prod_i y_i^{
u_i}$. Choosing exactly one $z_i$ gives $
u_i a y_i^{
u_i-1}\prod_{j
e i}y_j^{
u_j}z_i$, interpreted as zero for $
u_i=0$; this is precisely $e_y(d_i(a x^
u))z_i$ by [def-weil-pbc-polynomial-partial-derivative]. Every remaining term contains at least two $z$ factors, hence belongs to $I^{2s}T$ when all $z_i\in I^sT$. Add over the finitely many monomials of $p$ [def-weil-pbc-mv-polynomial-ring]. This proves $e_{y+z}(p)-e_y(p)-\sum_i e_y(d_i p)z_i\in I^{2s}T$; retaining all terms with at least one $z$ also proves $e_{y+z}(p)-e_y(p)\in I^sT$. This uses no division by factorials.
